WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
Speaker 1: Luke Beasley here filling in for&nbsp;
David while he is away on paternity leave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:08.080
We've a fascinating story to talk about. So as&nbsp;
has been widely covered over the last few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.960
Amazon's work environment is pretty horrible.&nbsp;
And there's been a lot of stories revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.160
about people not being able to go to the restroom&nbsp;
properly, like they're driving down the road and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.560
they have so little time that they're&nbsp;
expected to do something that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.880
going to the restroom, you know,&nbsp;
in their car, in a bag or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.800
being expected to do way too much and not&nbsp;
being treated correctly for it. And one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.280
the reasons that this work environment was the&nbsp;
way that it was was actually sort of intentional.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.440
Amazon, higher ups had designed it where you get&nbsp;
pushed so hard that there's no way you're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.280
stay very long and they're okay with that. They're&nbsp;
going to have a really high turnover rate. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.640
then while you're there, extract as much work as&nbsp;
possible and not treat you the way that you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.560
be treated. So they go through a massive amount of&nbsp;
employees each month and each year who just go in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.520
they go out, they go in, they go out and they&nbsp;
grind them down so aggressively and so quickly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.040
So besides public pressure, it was actually&nbsp;
working for the higher ups. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.720
if you think from their perspective that we're&nbsp;
getting a lot of work out of these people and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:17.200
making huge profits until now. And this is wild&nbsp;
reading from Yahoo! Finance. Amazon's no frills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.360
low wage, high turnover labor model is&nbsp;
beginning to show signs of stress. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.520
tech giant could run out of workers&nbsp;
to hire for its warehouses by 2024,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.680
according to a leaked Amazon internal research&nbsp;
memo from May 2021, seen by the publication Recode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.720
putting its service quality growth plans&nbsp;
and quick labor churn model in a pinch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.240
If we continue business as usual, Amazon will&nbsp;
deplete the available labor supply in the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:48.800
network by 2024, the leaked report indicated. So&nbsp;
because they push their workers so hard, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:55.120
literally going to be in a situation where at all&nbsp;
these given warehouses, there's all the vicinity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:01.440
around where they can hire from, aren't going&nbsp;
to have enough employees, potential employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.560
who are within the type of employee that would&nbsp;
work that particular position who haven't already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.880
left Amazon. Is that insane? So what is their&nbsp;
solution? Well, reading again from Yahoo! Finance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.440
the memo said Amazon had six levers it could&nbsp;
use to delay the labor crisis by a few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.120
including raising wages and increasing automation.&nbsp;
But the only way to significantly alter this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.640
timeline is to make sweeping changes to the&nbsp;
way it manages its employees. So obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.000
automation could possibly help them with this,&nbsp;
take away some of the jobs that they need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.920
but is not going to do it all. And so I love how&nbsp;
they stumbled into what we've been all screaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.240
at them for a long time now. You were one of&nbsp;
the biggest corporations in the world. You make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.640
tons of money. You can afford to pay your workers&nbsp;
correctly and treat them correctly. And it's crazy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.080
because they are just now realizing, hey, if we&nbsp;
want people to work here, do we have to not abuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:56.000
them to be decent to them? Is that something&nbsp;
that we have to do? Yes. Unbelievable. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.520
I hope that they go in this direction, of course,&nbsp;
and treat all the workers the way that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.240
deserve to be treated. Wish they could have&nbsp;
figured this out earlier, but we'll take it now.

